WEBVTT

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:12.659
Equity practice grows the way most professional skill does, unevenly
and through real work, so it helps to pause now and then and notice

00:00:12.659 --> 00:00:13.873
where you are.

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:18.296
Near the top of My work you will find a panel called Your learning
journey.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:23.863
The site describes it as a place to notice how far you have come and
to choose where to go next.

00:00:23.986 --> 00:00:31.446
It is private to you, nothing in it is graded, and you remain
welcome to explore anything on the site that catches your interest.

00:00:32.012 --> 00:00:36.193
The panel begins with one question: Where are you right now?

00:00:36.316 --> 00:00:40.470
Four levels describe common stages in equity practice.

00:00:40.592 --> 00:00:46.607
Getting oriented fits when you are new to the Disability Services
Division, or new to equity work.

00:00:46.729 --> 00:00:51.368
It helps you see what the program offers and where authority for
decisions sits.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:59.599
Foundations is about building a shared language with colleagues, so
you can talk about equity, access and bias without labeling people.

00:00:59.721 --> 00:01:07.132
Applying means bringing equity into real decisions, like an
analysis, a hiring process or a service change.

00:01:07.254 --> 00:01:11.554
Leading and sustaining is for shaping systems and helping others
grow.

00:01:12.121 --> 00:01:15.228
The advice is to pick the one that feels closest.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:22.050
You can change it whenever you like, and the site notes that
experienced staff often start at the third or fourth level.

00:01:22.172 --> 00:01:27.010
The panel is plain that these levels are not a ladder you are
required to climb.

00:01:27.132 --> 00:01:33.418
Once you choose, a short list called A few good next steps appears,
with links suited to that level.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:41.340
Someone getting oriented might start with a ten-minute Equity Pause,
while someone at Applying will see courses and a guided practice for

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.551
equity analysis.

00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:48.573
Below the next steps, What you can do now offers a few I can
statements for your level.

00:01:48.696 --> 00:01:57.082
At Foundations, for example, you might check that you can use plain
language about equity, access and bias, recognize a barrier without

00:01:57.082 --> 00:01:59.929
labeling people, and ask before assuming.

00:02:00.051 --> 00:02:04.860
Each level also includes one milestone marked Try it in your work.

00:02:04.982 --> 00:02:08.078
Check these off when they feel true for you.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.500
In the panel's own words, your own judgment is the only measure.

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:17.356
Sometimes it helps to talk about your growth with a supervisor or
mentor.

00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:24.552
For that, Download a summary creates a one-page Word document with
your current level, the statements you have checked, and the

00:02:24.552 --> 00:02:26.746
practice plans you have worked on.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:35.228
It closes by saying you are sharing it by choice, as your own view
of your growth, to start a conversation rather than to evaluate you.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.537
Moving to a new computer?

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:43.470
Choose Save a copy, then use Open a saved copy on the new one to
pick up where you left off.

00:02:44.037 --> 00:02:50.256
The same four level names appear across the site, so you can spot
material that suits where you are.

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:54.008
Many resources and courses show a level near the top.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:03:01.176
On this checklist for plain language and accessible documents, you
will see Foundations, and selecting it takes you to the matching

00:03:01.176 --> 00:03:02.874
section of Learning paths.

00:03:02.997 --> 00:03:07.650
A level on a resource is a suggestion about where it tends to help
most.

00:03:07.772 --> 00:03:12.177
It is guidance rather than a gate, and anyone can open anything.

00:03:12.743 --> 00:03:15.977
Under Learn, the Paths page brings this together.

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:24.052
Learning paths groups five guided pathways and nine reading paths
under Foundations, Applying, and Leading and sustaining.

00:03:24.174 --> 00:03:31.795
Every path begins with a question from real work, such as whose
ideas actually shape decisions in a meeting that is open to

00:03:31.795 --> 00:03:32.652
everyone.

00:03:32.774 --> 00:03:39.848
The page calls this grouping a guide rather than a gate, so follow
whichever path speaks to your question today.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:44.169
If you are new to DSD, it suggests getting to know the program
first.

00:03:44.736 --> 00:03:48.458
It is worth being clear about what your learning journey is not.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.057
It is never an evaluation.

00:03:51.179 --> 00:03:56.308
It is private to you, and your choices are kept only in this browser
on this computer.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.171
None of it is sent to your supervisor or to program staff.

00:04:00.293 --> 00:04:07.171
Because it stays on the computer, anyone using that same computer
could see it, so keep that in mind on a shared device.

00:04:07.293 --> 00:04:14.348
As a next step, open My work, choose the level that feels closest
today, and try one suggested step this week.
